.TH H264\-SORT 1 "2026-10-25" "h264-sort" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
h264\-sort \- move H.264/AVC videos into an h.264 subdirectory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B h264\-sort
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIDIR\fR|\fIFILE\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B h264\-sort
examines the video files in the given directories and moves every file whose
first video stream is H.264/AVC into a subdirectory of the same directory,
named
.B h.264
by default. Files with any other codec (HEVC, AV1, MPEG\-2, ...) are left where
they are.
.P
The codec is determined with
.BR ffprobe (1)
from the first video stream, not from the file name or extension, so a
.B .mkv
that actually contains H.264 is detected correctly and a mislabelled container
is not mistaken for H.264.
.P
Probing an MPEG\-TS over a network mount costs about a second, so the scan is
deliberately not per file. Captures from one model always share a codec, and
the model is readable from the file name, so
.B h264\-sort
groups the candidates by model, probes at most one file per model in parallel,
and caches the answer in
.BR ~/.cache/h264\-sort/codecs.tsv .
A later run over the same models probes nothing and only moves files. Deleting
that cache file is always safe; it is rebuilt on demand.
.P
Files are moved, never copied, and an existing destination file is never
overwritten: a numeric suffix
.RI ( name.1.ext )
is used instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-codec " CODEC"
Collect files whose first video stream is
.I CODEC
as reported by
.BR ffprobe (1)
(default:
.BR h264 ).
The cache stores every codec it sees, so switching
.B \-c
costs no new probes.
.TP
.BI \-t ", " \-\-target " NAME"
Use
.I NAME
as the destination subdirectory instead of
.BR h.264 .
The name defaults to
.B h.264
when the codec is
.BR h264 ,
and to the codec name otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Also search subdirectories. Each directory that contains matches gets its own
target subdirectory; the target directories themselves are pruned and never
searched.
.TP
.BI \-j ", " \-\-jobs " N"
Probe
.I N
models concurrently (default: 4). Only matters on a cold cache.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-refresh
Probe every model found in this run again and update the cache, ignoring what
is already stored. Cached models that are not part of this run are kept.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be moved without changing anything on disk. New probe results
are still cached.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.SS Candidate files
Only these extensions are considered, case\-insensitively:
.BR mkv ,
.BR mp4 ,
.BR mov ,
.BR m4v ,
.BR avi ,
.BR ts ,
.BR mts ,
.BR m2ts ,
.BR webm ,
.BR flv ,
.BR wmv ,
.BR mpg ,
.BR mpeg ,
.BR ogv ,
.BR 3gp .
Without
.B \-r
only the top level of each directory is scanned.
.SS Model grouping
A model is the file name prefix in front of the capture timestamp,
.IR _YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS_mmm .
Model names may contain underscores and the separator may be one or two of
them, so the timestamp is matched rather than splitting on
.BR _ :
.B maye_wells__2026-10-22_18-45-04_225.ts
and
.B nikoru_sp_2026-10-23_13-06-41_790.ts
group as
.B maye_wells
and
.BR nikoru_sp .
A file without a recognisable timestamp is its own group and is probed
individually.
.SS Detection
The representative file of each unprobed model is run through
.BR ffprobe (1)
with
.B \-select_streams v:0
and
.BR \-show_entries stream=codec_name .
Files with no video stream, unreadable files, and files whose codec does not
match are counted as skipped and left untouched. Probe results are written as
the run proceeds, so interrupting with
.B Ctrl\-C
does not lose the models already probed.
.SS Requirements
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ffprobe (1)
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.cache/h264\-sort/codecs.tsv
Model to codec cache, tab\-separated. Honours
.BR XDG_CACHE_HOME .
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview what would be moved in a media folder:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-n /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.P
Move the H.264 files, leaving HEVC where it is:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.P
Handle a folder tree, including nested captures:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-r /mnt/data_storage/Good
.EE
.P
Gather HEVC files instead, into a folder named
.BR hevc ,
probing eight models at a time:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-c hevc \-t hevc \-r \-j 8 /mnt/data_storage/Good
.EE
.P
Re-probe everything, for example after a model changed codec:
.IP
.EX
h264\-sort \-R \-n /mnt/data_storage/vr
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No move failed. Skipped files (not the requested codec) do not count as
failures.
.TP
.B 1
At least one move failed.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage (no directory or file given, invalid
.BR \-j ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ts2mkv (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written for local use.
